						 <div style="text-align: right;"></div> <p><strong>Item #:</strong> SCP-5187</p> <p><strong>Object Class:</strong> Euclid</p> <p><strong>Special Containment Procedures:</strong> Instances of SCP-5187 are to be kept in a standard anomalous object containment locker. If an instance of SCP-5187 or SCP-5187-1 is confirmed, a search of book vendors within 10km of the instances is to be performed; if additional instances of SCP-5187 are discovered they are to be obtained and taken into containment. Regular searches of online vendors are also to be conducted.</p> <p>Currently, known examples of SCP-5187-1 have been deemed <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">a negligible</span> moderate threat to Foundation goals or personnel. However, should it become necessary to detain them, a standard humanoid containment cell with <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">a single guard is sufficient.</span> two guards assigned to duty at all times. The guard may <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">converse with the SCP-5187-1 instance on any matters unrelated to the function of the containment site.</span> not have verbal contact with instances of SCP-5187-1. Cells containing instances of SCP-5187-1 are to be thoroughly inspected once daily for unauthorized materials or debris.</p> <p><strong>Description:</strong> SCP-5187 is a paperback book of the title <em>The Whole Art of Deduction</em>, however instances of SCP-5187 have been located with minor alterations to the title. Additionally, the author is credited variously under names associated with the fictional detective Sherlock Holmes. Efforts to trace the origins of these books have succeeded only in uncovering now-defunct publishing companies and publication dates for each book consistent with the final year of operation of each company.</p> <p>The contents of the book are a collection of clinical writings on the subject of observation and drawing inferences based on one’s observations in a manner consistent with displays seen within the Sherlock Holmes stories. Other subjects touched upon involve building the reader’s knowledge base and encouragement to keep up with news.</p> <p>The anomalous properties of SCP-5187 will begin to emerge as it is read to completion by a human<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-988383-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" >1</a></sup>, hereafter referred to as SCP-5187-1.</p> <p>SCP-5187-1 instances are created from those who have read SCP-5187. Behaviors displayed include a treatment of the book’s authorship<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-988383-2" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" >2</a></sup> as fact and frequent reference to it as one would a study resource. While many SCP-5187-1 subjects will display higher scores in cognitive testing this may be attributable to internalizing the advice within the book. However, with frequency that grows in proportion to how far into the object an SCP-5187-1 subject has read they display an ability to deduce, from apparently minor and insignificant details, unerringly accurate and expansive data. They are, additionally, shown to display a draw towards sensational or lurid crimes in an effort to solve them.</p> <p>Known instances of SCP-5187-1 have been seen to reject, outright, the premise of supernatural forces.</p> <div class="collapsible-block"> <div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Recovery</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none"> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Recovery</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-content"> <p>The first instance of SCP-5187 was discovered on 03/29/1990 in the possession of Greg Athelney, the head of a Sherlockian society known as <em>The Veiled Lodgers</em>. By the time of recovery Mr. Athelney<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-988383-3" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" >3</a></sup> had taken a plane from Somerset, England to Boston, Massachusetts. Reason for travel were interest in both the reward offered in the aftermath of the Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum theft and an effort to test himself against &quot;the master&quot;, presumably Sherlock Holmes.</p> <p>An insistence on consulting with local police and behavior generally considered obstructive brought him to the attention of embedded Foundation agents within Boston Police Department. When it accurately inferred an agent as representing a private interest, it was dubbed a possibly hostile POI and taken into Foundation custody for a period to determine whether it was involved with any other GOI.</p> <p>The end result was the determination by memetic researchers that much of SCP-5187-1A’s behavior was non-anomalous eccentricity, except for occasional sharp inferences which were linked to his ownership of SCP-5187. Foundation personnel were able to purchase SCP-5187 from SCP-5187-1A under the explanation that it would be made into an exhibit at the London Sherlock Holmes museum.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div class="collapsible-block"> <div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Continued&nbsp;contact</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none"> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Continued&nbsp;contact</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-content"> <p>As of 5/15/90 it has been determined that SCP-5187-1A has begun to investigate the Foundation after being apprehended.</p> <p>7/12/90, SCP-5187-1A has managed to breach containment. The accepted chain of events involved prolonged interaction with his guard to learn necessary details of scheduling to effect an escape. Precise details remain under investigation at this time, however a likely scenario has been put together:<br /> SCP-5187-1A appears to have spent the intervening time between its initial detainment and escape in frequent conversation with the guard to its cell 'to pass the time' as it insisted. In this way, it developed a rapport and some degree of trust with him. Additionally, discovery of molded food in SCP-5187-1A's cell has lead to the belief it hoarded portions of meals it was served, keeping them hidden while cultivating the eventual growth of mold. Exposing itself to the mold SCP-5187-1A intentionally made itself ill enough that the guard opened the cell to examine it.<br /> It was then that SCP-5187-1A managed to take the guard sufficiently unaware to incapacitate him and, utilizing the uniform and equipment on the guard's person effect his escape.</p> <p>Containment procedures are undergoing modification to reflect these events.</p> <p>8/1/90, as of the most recent writing SCP-5187-1A has made no efforts to contact friends or family, nor returned to their place of residence. SCP-5187-1A's whereabouts are currently unknown.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div class="collapsible-block"> <div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Interview&nbsp;Log</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none"> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Interview&nbsp;Log</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-content"> <div class="blockquote"> <p><strong>Interviewed:</strong> SCP-5187-1A</p> <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Dr. Jeremy Bell</p> <p><strong>Foreword:</strong> Subject of Interview, SCP-5187-1A's apparent search for the Foundation</p> <p><strong>&lt;Begin Log&gt;</strong></p> <p><strong>Dr. Jeremy Bell:</strong> Mr. Athelney, I would like to ask you a few questions if I may.</p> <p><strong>SCP-5187-1A:</strong> I would imagine I haven't much choice, do I?</p> <p><strong>Dr. Jeremy Bell:</strong> On the contrary, you're more than welcome to maintain silence, but it would do you more harm than good in the long run. But, if you're feeling talkative, what were you hoping to achieve when you were apprehended?</p> <p><strong>SCP-5187-1A:</strong> [Spreads his arms] Information. Knowledge. The story you gave me about working for the Sherlock Holmes museum was a lie. In the first place, I doubt that the museum would have been willing to part with the sum of money I demanded for my copy of the book when there must certainly be other copies and likely far less dog-eared than my own. That was my second indication that something was wrong. First, naturally, was why museum administrators would need to be carrying firearms under their jackets. I would recommend you have them button their coats better in the future.<br /> Of course, confirming my suspicions was as simple as sneaking into the museum after hours and looking through their inventory. I can only presume that you gentlemen are associated with the ones I ran into across the pond.</p> <p><strong>Dr. Jeremy Bell:</strong> That's a fairly impressive conclusion to come to.</p> <p><strong>SCP-5187-1A:</strong> I believe you mean elementary. The facts I've already laid out make it obvious.</p> <p><strong>Dr. Jeremy Bell:</strong> Back to the question at hand, then. What information were you looking for?</p> <p><strong>SCP-5187-1A:</strong> Who you are. What you're doing. You had men in place in a police force in the United States. You have representatives in Britain and a sizable detainment facility. You aren't government, that much is certain.</p> <p><strong>Dr. Jeremy Bell:</strong> No. No we aren't. But I'm afraid that's all I'm authorized to tell you.</p> <p><strong>SCP-5187-1A:</strong> You've already told me more than enough, Doctor. You've given me threads, I need only pull on them and see what comes loose. Are we finished for today?</p> <p><strong>Dr. Jeremy Bell:</strong> I believe so. I may speak with you again, though.</p> <p><strong>SCP-5187-1A:</strong> I'll likely still be here a while longer.</p> <p><strong>&lt;End Log&gt;</strong></p> </div> </div> </div> </div> <div class="collapsible-block"> <div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Memo&nbsp;The&nbsp;debacle</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none"> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;The&nbsp;debacle</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-content"> <p><em>How? How did the SCP escape from under our noses? The entire plan was absurd! I want full investigation into the entire duty roster around SCP-5187-1A and surveillance on all of his known associates. I don't care how ridiculous his demeanor appears, this man is dangerous and has entirely too much information as it is. We can't afford to leave him on the run.<br /> -Site Director Hopkins</em></p> <p><em>I take full responsibility for the laxness in the containment procedures and will be personally revising them. I cannot begin to express my regret at how this turned out and will submit to whatever disciplinary measures are deemed appropriate.<br /> -Dr. Jeremy Bell</em></p> </div> </div> </div> <div class="collapsible-block"> <div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Memo&nbsp;Site&nbsp;Director&nbsp;Hopkins</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none"> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Cocky,&nbsp;isn't&nbsp;he?</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-content"> <p><em>While overseeing the search and cleaning out of SCP-5187-1A's former containment cell, among the scraps of food discovered were a few orange seeds. Exactly five, in fact. Unless I've misjudged our friend very much, I think he meant them as a final reference to Sherlock Holmes; he used such a theatrical touch to warn a secretive organization that he was on their trail.<br /> I believe it safe to say that he is not simply in hiding, but will continue actively seeking to expose the Foundation.<br /> It is my current recommendation that when SCP-5187-1A is recovered, they be amnesticized and kept under strict observation.<br /> -Dr. Jeremy Bell</em></p> </div> </div> </div> <p>by <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mr-lapin" ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5968622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605794337" alt="Mr Lapin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5968622)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mr-lapin" >Mr Lapin</a></span></p> <div class="footnotes-footer"> <div class="title">Footnotes</div> <div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-988383-1"><a href="javascript:;" >1</a>. Testing with other sapients pending approval</div> <div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-988383-2"><a href="javascript:;" >2</a>. Often attributed to Sherlock Holmes himself.</div> <div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-988383-3"><a href="javascript:;" >3</a>. Hereafter SCP-5187-1A</div> </div> 
